<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Auvergne</h1><p class="st-meta">980 CE – 1531 CE (approx.) · capital Clermont</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The county of the volcanic Massif Central, centred on Clermont, where Pope Urban II preached the First Crusade in 1095.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Auvergne was ruled in the late 9th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-duke-of-aquitaine">William the Pious</a> as part of his Aquitanian power. In the later 10th century the viscounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont</a> took the comital title, and by around 980 they governed the county as hereditary counts under the loose overlordship of the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was a land of uplands, pasture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> churches, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont</a> a rival power in the city. In 1095 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Urban II</a> proclaimed the First Crusade at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>. In the 12th century a family dispute split off the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dauphine-of-viennois">Dauphiné</a> of Auvergne, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II</a>'s campaigns from 1209 seized much of the county for the crown.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The remaining county, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vic-le-comte">Vic-le-Comte</a>, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour</a> family and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeleine-de-la-tour-dauvergne">Madeleine de la Tour d'Auvergne</a> to her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>. The royal lands of Auvergne, held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, were confiscated after 1523 and united with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> in 1531.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-auvergne.jpg" alt="County of Auvergne" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_de_l%27Auvergne.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>980 CE – 1531 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Clermont</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William the Pious, Guy II of Auvergne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Auvergne</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1256&amp;focus=county-of-auvergne" aria-label="County of Auvergne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4736 456 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Auvergne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5056 712L5054 728M5056 712L5035 888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5035 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5056 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5056 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Clermont. <a href="/atlas?year=1256&amp;focus=county-of-auvergne">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
